In trapezoid $ABCD$  we have $\overline{AB}$   parallel to $\overline{DC}$, $E$  as the midpoint of $\overline{BC}$, and $F$ as the midpoint of $\overline{DA}$. The area of $ABEF$ is twice the area of $FECD$. What is $AB/DC$?
Solution: First note that $FE = (AB + DC)/2$. Because trapezoids $ABEF$ and $FECD$ have the same height, the ratio of their areas is equal to the ratio of the averages of their parallel sides. Since \[
AB + \frac{AB+DC}{2} = \frac{3 AB + DC}{2}
\]and \[
\frac{AB+DC}{2} + DC = \frac{AB + 3 DC}{2},
\]we have \[
3AB + DC = 2(AB + 3DC) = 2AB + 6DC, \quad \text{and} \quad \frac{AB}{DC} = \boxed{5}.
\][asy]
pair A,B,C,D,I,F;
A=(0,0);
B=(13,0);
F=(2,4);
D=(4,8);
I=(10,4);
C=(7,8);
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(0.7));
draw(I--F,linewidth(0.7));
label("$A$",A,W);
label("$B$",B,E);
label("$C$",C,E);
label("$D$",D,W);
label("$F$",F,W);
label("$E$",I,E);
[/asy]

Final steps of a worked solution: Because trapezoids $ABEF$ and $FECD$ have the same height, the ratio of their areas is equal to the ratio of the averages of their parallel sides. Since \[
AB + \frac{AB+DC}{2} = \frac{3 AB + DC}{2}
\]and \[
\frac{AB+DC}{2} + DC = \frac{AB + 3 DC}{2},
\]we have \[
3AB + DC = 2(AB + 3DC) = 2AB + 6DC, \quad \text{and} \quad \frac{AB}{DC} = \boxed{5}.
\][asy]
pair A,B,C,D,I,F;
A=(0,0);
B=(13,0);
F=(2,4);
D=(4,8);
I=(10,4);
C=(7,8);
draw(A--B--C--D--cycle,linewidth(0.7));
draw(I--F,linewidth(0.7));
label("$A$",A,W);
label("$B$",B,E);
label("$C$",C,E);
label("$D$",D,W);
label("$F$",F,W);
label("$E$",I,E);
[/asy]

